{"reference":"John 9","book":"John","chapter":9,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Interpretive Questions","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter directly rejects the disciples’ attempt to explain the man’s blindness as punishment for either his own sin or his parents’ sin. That correction should not turn the man’s disability into merely an object lesson or a cure narrative.\n\nVerse 22 explains the parents’ caution by referring to exclusion from the synagogue for confessing Jesus as the Christ. This notice has been used in reconstructions of conflict between Johannine believers and synagogue communities, but the text alone does not establish one late first-century decree or a single, uniform break. That larger historical reconstruction remains disputed.","verse_refs":["John 9:1-41","John 9:22"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_john_009_403eb3bc0924ee0720d73460"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}